    "summary": "Mellow first set, soaring second‑set segues with stellar Wharf Rat>Half‑Step jam",
    "blurb": "The concert opens warm and mellow, highlighted by Keith’s piano on the Weather Report Suite and a relaxed first‑set flow. The second set launches into an epic sequence – Wharf Rat → Mississippi Half‑Step → Playing In The Band → Mind Left Body Jam → He's Gone → Truckin' – capped with a moving Morning Dew encore.",
    "review": "December 2, 1973 at Boston Music Hall finds the Dead in a warm, dark‑mellow groove that builds into one of ’73’s most memorable second sets. Keith Godchaux’s piano shines on the Weather Report Suite Prelude and Part 1, laying down a lush backdrop for Jerry Garcia’s lyrical guitar work that ranges from delicate phrasing on “Brown Eyed Women” to fiery riffs during the post‑Playin’ jam. Phil Lesh battles “giant dinosaurs” on the Half‑Step, anchoring the band as they weave through an astonishing chain of segues: Wharf Rat → Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo → Playing In The Band → Mind Left Body Jam → He's Gone → Truckin' → Stella Blue, each transition feeling both seamless and exploratory. Bob Weir’s vocals tighten up on the second‑set numbers, while Bill Kreutzmann provides a steady, propulsive pulse that drives the jams forward. The encore’s “Morning Dew” delivers an emotional close, making this show a standout example of late‑year 1973 improvisational brilliance captured in several high‑quality recordings.",
